{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And these are the judgments.</b> Rashi explained: everywhere that 'these' is said, it disqualifies the preceding ones, etc. From his commentary it implies that had it written here 'these,' it would imply disqualifying the preceding ones, and I would say they were not said at Sinai. But this is astonishing, for it is written above: ״וירד ה׳ על הר סיני״. Therefore it seems to me: פסל is from the language of ״פסל לך״ — like the work of something hewn and separated from its fellow. And this is the meaning: had it written 'these,' I would say that the latter ones are distinct and separated from the former ones, and I would say that the latter ones were not said at Sinai. Therefore it says 'and these,' adding to the first matter. And 'et' is like 'min,' like ״כצאתי את העיר״. And likewise ״אלה דברי הברית״ at the end of the portion כי תבא is separated and distinct from its fellow, for the former are words of rebuke and curse and the latter are blessing. At the beginning of the book of Mishneh Torah, where it is written ״אלה הדברים״, the matter of 'disqualifying' does not apply there, because it is written at the beginning of the book. ״אלה תולדות השמים והארץ״ in Genesis disqualifies the preceding ones — tohu and vohu." "<b>Before them.</b> Rashi explained: and not before gentile courts, etc. — even if they judge him according to Israelite law. A parable: two sick people, and the doctor came to visit them. He said about one: feed him and give him drink whatever he wants; and about this one: guard him from such-and-such food. They said to him: on the contrary, this one is more endangered than that one. What did he say? He said to them: it follows from what I know — because I know that he is sick unto death, I said: do not withhold anything from him, for whether he eats or does not eat he will die. But this one, who stands to live, I said: let him not eat such-and-such a thing, lest his illness worsen. So too with the statutes of the nations it is written: ״ואתן להם חקים לא טובים ומשפטים לא יחיו בהם״. But with Israel it is written: ״ושמרתם את חקתי אשר יעשה אותם האדם וחי בהם״ etc. And this is also the reason He forbade us creeping things but not the nations, as it says: ״מגיד דבריו ליעקב חקיו ומשפטיו לישראל״ etc. ״לא עשה כן לכל גוי״ etc.\nAnd there was an incident with Onkelos the convert, the nephew of the emperor Hadrian, who sought to convert and was afraid of his uncle. He said to him: I wish to engage in commerce. He said to him: do you perhaps lack silver and gold? My treasuries are before you. He said to him: I wish to go abroad and gain wisdom to know the peoples. Give advice: in what merchandise? He said to him: that which you see has fallen into decline but is destined to rise — and you will profit. He went to the land of Israel and asked the sages to learn Torah before being circumcised. They said to him: the words of Torah do not endure except in one who is circumcised. He went and was circumcised, and learned much Torah. Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Joshua found him and saw his face was changed. They said to one another: Onkelos has learned Torah. As soon as he came to them, he began to ask many questions. He went to his uncle Hadrian. He said to him: why is your face changed? He said to him: because I learned Torah — and not only that, but I was circumcised. He said to him: and who counseled you thus? He said to him: you did, for you told me to engage in merchandise that has fallen but is destined to rise. And I went around to every nation and did not find a nation as lowly as Israel, yet destined to rise, as Isaiah said (Isaiah 49:7): ״כה אמר ה׳ גואל ישראל וקדושו לבזה נפש למתעב גוי לעבד מושלים מלכים יראו וקמו״. He struck him on the cheek. He turned back and said to him: why did you do so? He said to him: because I sought to learn Torah. He said to him: you should have learned Torah and not been circumcised. He said to him: it is impossible to learn without circumcision, as it says: ״מגיד דבריו ליעקב חקיו ומשפטיו לישראל״ — to one who is circumcised like Jacob (born circumcised). And it is written: ״לא עשה כן לכל גוי ומשפטים בל ידעום״ — בל, they are unable to learn Torah, which begins with bet and ends with lamed: ״לעיני כל ישראל״."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:47.538624046Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}